WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.151
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online English for Academic Purposes.

2
00:00:06.451 --> 00:00:09.722
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.022 --> 00:00:16.134
Unit eleven: Avoiding Plagiarism. Today you will use sources honestly and reference them correctly.

4
00:00:16.434 --> 00:00:19.156
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.519
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:25.419
Plagiarism.

7
00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:29.078
Plagiarism means using someone's work as your own.

8
00:00:29.683 --> 00:00:31.242
Plagiarism is serious.

9
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:34.386
Your turn. Say: Plagiarism.

10
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:38.142
Plagiarism.

11
00:00:38.883 --> 00:00:39.966
Citation.

12
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.623
Citation means a note naming a source.

13
00:00:44.217 --> 00:00:45.485
Add a citation.

14
00:00:46.083 --> 00:00:48.968
Your turn. Say: Citation.

15
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:52.869
Citation.

16
00:00:53.617 --> 00:00:54.629
Quotation.

17
00:00:55.617 --> 00:00:58.394
Quotation means exact words from a source.

18
00:00:58.983 --> 00:01:00.739
Use quotation marks.

19
00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.223
Your turn. Say: Quotation.

20
00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.079
Quotation.

21
00:01:08.817 --> 00:01:09.760
Source.

22
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.075
Source means where information comes from.

23
00:01:13.683 --> 00:01:15.010
Name your source.

24
00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:17.874
Your turn. Say: Source.

25
00:01:20.733 --> 00:01:21.885
Source.

26
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:23.442
Author.

27
00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:26.589
Author means the writer of a text.

28
00:01:27.183 --> 00:01:28.510
The author is Smith.

29
00:01:29.117 --> 00:01:31.305
Your turn. Say: Author.

30
00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:35.052
Author.

31
00:01:35.817 --> 00:01:37.073
Bibliography.

32
00:01:38.083 --> 00:01:40.721
Bibliography means a list of sources.

33
00:01:41.317 --> 00:01:42.852
Write a bibliography.

34
00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:46.079
Your turn. Say: Bibliography.

35
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:50.271
Bibliography.

36
00:01:51.017 --> 00:01:51.809
Credit.

37
00:01:52.817 --> 00:01:54.665
Credit means to give recognition.

38
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:56.390
Credit the author.

39
00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.357
Your turn. Say: Credit.

40
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.120
Credit.

41
00:02:03.883 --> 00:02:04.838
Original.

42
00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:08.117
Original means new and not copied.

43
00:02:08.717 --> 00:02:10.519
Submit original work.

44
00:02:11.117 --> 00:02:13.363
Your turn. Say: Original.

45
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.131
Original.

46
00:02:17.933 --> 00:02:21.735
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:22.583 --> 00:02:24.664
Visal, what counts as plagiarism?

48
00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:35.401
Copying text without quotation marks, using someone's idea without naming them and submitting another student's work are all plagiarism.

49
00:02:36.517 --> 00:02:37.680
How do I avoid it?

50
00:02:38.783 --> 00:02:48.936
Paraphrase in your own words and cite the source. If you copy exact words, put them in quotation marks and add the author and year.

51
00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:51.945
What happens if I plagiarise?

52
00:02:53.050 --> 00:03:01.661
Universities treat it seriously. You can lose marks or even fail the course. Honest work and good references protect you.

53
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.138
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:04.983 --> 00:03:08.155
Grammar focus: Reporting verbs with sources.

55
00:03:08.455 --> 00:03:09.631
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:10.317 --> 00:03:13.872
Smith (2020) argues that sleep improves memory.

57
00:03:14.783 --> 00:03:17.863
According to Lee, students need regular breaks.

58
00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:26.189
Use reporting verbs such as argues, states, suggests and claims followed by that. You can also use According to plus the author.

59
00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:30.763
Be careful! Don't say: Smith argue that sleep helps.

60
00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.350
Say: Smith argues that sleep helps.

61
00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:37.220
Add s for one author.

62
00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:41.046
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:41.833 --> 00:03:43.264
An Honest Essay.

64
00:03:43.564 --> 00:03:46.098
Thida writes an essay using three articles.

65
00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.831
She paraphrases the ideas and names each author.

66
00:03:50.833 --> 00:03:53.773
She puts one exact sentence in quotation marks.

67
00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:58.020
At the end she adds a bibliography of all sources.

68
00:03:59.033 --> 00:04:01.741
She submits her original work with confidence.

69
00:04:02.833 --> 00:04:06.591
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:10.262
Plagiarism means using others' work as blank.

71
00:04:10.562 --> 00:04:14.030
A, a gift. B, a map. C, your own.

72
00:04:17.567 --> 00:04:19.195
The answer is C: your own.

73
00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:22.709
Exact words need quotation blank.

74
00:04:23.009 --> 00:04:26.431
A, marks. B, books. C, rooms.

75
00:04:29.967 --> 00:04:31.792
The answer is A: marks.

76
00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:35.135
A bibliography is a list of blank.

77
00:04:35.435 --> 00:04:39.042
A, grades. B, sources. C, friends.

78
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:44.739
The answer is B: sources.

79
00:04:45.883 --> 00:04:47.558
To credit means to give blank.

80
00:04:47.858 --> 00:04:51.720
A, a test. B, a prize. C, recognition.

81
00:04:55.267 --> 00:04:57.870
The answer is C: recognition.

82
00:04:59.017 --> 00:05:00.703
Thida submits blank work.

83
00:05:01.003 --> 00:05:04.227
A, original. B, copied. C, late.

84
00:05:07.767 --> 00:05:09.708
The answer is A: original.

85
00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:14.179
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:15.017 --> 00:05:16.900
How do you avoid plagiarism?

87
00:05:25.717 --> 00:05:27.461
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:27.761 --> 00:05:42.595
I paraphrase ideas in my own words and cite every source. If I use exact words I put them in quotation marks. At the end I write a bibliography of all my sources.

89
00:05:42.895 --> 00:05:44.604
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:05:45.517 --> 00:05:50.096
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and reporting verbs with sources.

91
00:05:50.396 --> 00:05:55.150
Today's value is integrity. Do the right thing, even when no one is watching.

92
00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:58.137
See you in Unit twelve. Goodbye!

93
00:05:58.437 --> 00:06:01.345
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

